@{
    Layout = "~/Areas/GitHub/Views/Shared/GithubLayout.cshtml";
    ViewData["Title"] = "Cloning a Repository";
}

<main id="tutorials" class="container py-1">
    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- Sidebar Navigation -->
        <aside class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/GitHub/Views/Shared/GithubTopics.cshtml")
        </aside>

        <!-- Reading Content Area -->
        <section class="col-lg-9">
            <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
                <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

                    <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">Cloning a Repository / Project</h1>

                    <p>
                        It downloads a copy of an existing remote Git repository,
                        such as a repository hosted on GitHub or GitLab, to your
                        local computer. It is usually the first step when
                        starting work on an existing project.
                    </p>
                    <p>The correct command is <code>git clone</code>.</p>

                    <p class="text-muted">
                        Learn how to get a repository URL, clone a project
                        using Command Prompt or GitHub Desktop, and understand
                        the difference between cloning and downloading a ZIP.
                    </p>

                    <hr class="my-4" />

                    <!-- Section 1 -->
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">1. Get the Repository URL from GitHub</h3>

                    <ol>
                        <li>Open the repository's main page on GitHub.</li>
                        <li>Click the green <strong>Code</strong> button.</li>
                        <li>Select the <strong>HTTPS</strong> tab.</li>
                        <li>Copy the repository URL.</li>
                    </ol>

                    <p>Example URL:</p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>https://github.com/username/project.git</code></pre>

                    <div class="alert alert-info">
                        <strong>Tip:</strong>
                        Replace the example URL with the URL copied from
                        the repository you want to clone.
                    </div>

                    <hr class="my-4" />

                    <!-- Section 2 -->
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">2. Clone Using Command Prompt (CMD)</h3>

                    <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">Step 1: Open the Destination Folder</h5>

                    <p>
                        Open Command Prompt and navigate to the directory
                        where you want to save the project.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>cd Documents\MyProjects</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        Make sure this directory exists before running the command.
                    </p>

                    <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">Step 2: Clone the Repository</h5>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git clone https://github.com/username/project.git</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        Git creates a new folder for the repository and
                        downloads its files and Git history.
                    </p>

                    <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">Step 3: Open the Project Folder</h5>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>cd project</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        You can now inspect the project or open it in your
                        preferred code editor.
                    </p>

                    <div class="alert alert-warning">
                        <strong>Note:</strong>
                        Git must be installed on your computer and available
                        from Command Prompt. Check this using
                        <code>git --version</code>.
                    </div>

                    <hr class="my-4" />

                    <!-- Section 3 -->
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">3. Clone Using GitHub Desktop</h3>

                    <p>
                        GitHub Desktop provides a graphical interface for
                        cloning repositories without typing Git commands.
                    </p>

                    <ol>
                        <li>Open the GitHub Desktop application.</li>
                        <li>Click <strong>File → Clone repository</strong>.</li>
                        <li>
                            Select the <strong>GitHub.com</strong> tab to
                            choose a listed repository, or select the
                            <strong>URL</strong> tab to paste a repository URL.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Choose the local path where you want to store
                            the project.
                        </li>
                        <li>Click <strong>Clone</strong>.</li>
                    </ol>

                    <p>
                        GitHub Desktop downloads the repository into your
                        selected local folder.
                    </p>

                    <hr class="my-4" />

                    <!-- Section 4 -->
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">4. What Happens When You Run <code>git clone</code>?</h3>

                    <p>Git automatically performs these main tasks:</p>

                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Downloads the project's files and available
                            repository history.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Creates a local Git repository with the
                            available branch and commit history.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Configures a remote named <code>origin</code>
                            pointing to the source repository.
                        </li>
                    </ol>

                    <p>Basic syntax:</p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git clone &lt;repository-url&gt;</code></pre>

                    <p>Example:</p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git clone https://github.com/username/nodeJSApplication.git</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        This creates a local folder named
                        <code>nodeJSApplication</code> containing the
                        repository's files and Git history.
                    </p>

                    <hr class="my-4" />

                    <!-- Section 5 -->
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">5. Git Clone vs Download ZIP</h3>

                    <p>
                        Downloading a ZIP is useful when you only need a
                        snapshot of the source code. Cloning is better when
                        you plan to develop the project, track changes,
                        or collaborate with others.
                    </p>

                    <div class="table-responsive">
                        <table class="table table-bordered table-striped align-middle">
                            <thead class="table-dark">
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Feature</th>
                                    <th>Download ZIP</th>
                                    <th>Git Clone</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Best For</strong></td>
                                    <td>One-time viewing or inspection</td>
                                    <td>Development and collaboration</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Git Connection</strong></td>
                                    <td>No Git remote configuration</td>
                                    <td>Configured with the source remote</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Getting Updates</strong></td>
                                    <td>Download another ZIP</td>
                                    <td>Use commands such as <code>git pull</code></td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Sending Updates</strong></td>
                                    <td>No Git history to push from the ZIP alone</td>
                                    <td>Can push commits if you have permission</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Commit History</strong></td>
                                    <td>Not included in the ZIP snapshot</td>
                                    <td>Repository history is available</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <div class="alert alert-success mt-4">
                        <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong>
                        Use <code>git clone</code> when you want a local
                        repository with its Git history and remote connection.
                        Download a ZIP when you only need the source files
                        for quick inspection.
                    </div>

                    
                    <!-- Previous / Next Navigation -->
                    <div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center gap-3 flex-wrap mt-5 pt-4 border-top">

                        <a class="btn btn-outline-primary"
                           href="@Url.Action("ConnectingRemotes", "RemoteCollaboration", new { area = "GitHub" })">
                            &larr; Previous: Connecting Remotes
                        </a>

                        <a class="btn btn-primary"
                           href="@Url.Action("PushingCode", "RemoteCollaboration", new { area = "GitHub" })">
                            Next: Pushing Code &rarr;
                        </a>

                    </div>


                </div>
            </div>
        </section>
    </div>
</main>
